#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069943 :tutorial/content "The **product rule for radicals** states that the square root of a product equals the product of the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sqrt{ab} &=\\sqrt{a} \\cdot \\sqrt{b}\n\t}\n$$\n\nWe can use the product rule to rewrite the square roots of algebraic expressions.\n\nAs an example, let's suppose we want to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{32y}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite $32y$ as the product $16 \\cdot 2y,$ which contains a perfect square $(16){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sqrt{32y}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\\sqrt{16 \\cdot 2y}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\\sqrt{4^2 \\cdot 2y}&\n\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we separate the product into two separate radicals using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n        \\sqrt{4^2\\cdot 2y}&=\\[3pt]\n        \\sqrt{4^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2y}&\n\t}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n        \\sqrt{4^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2y}&=\\[3pt]\n        |4|\\cdot \\sqrt{2y}&=\\[3pt]\n\n\t4\\sqrt{2y}&\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "169256a7-1898-59b1-a7a9-f8cb8b713d51" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1606 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
